// ── The `el` constructor family ────────────────────────────────────
//
// Shorthands for the HTML element vocabulary — the tree-shaped
// `HtmlFundamental` variants that templates and `lower`s build by the
// hundred. Each constructor is exactly its long form with the field
// names dropped:
//
//   el("div", ["book-toc-row"], kids)
//   HtmlFundamental::Element { tag: "div", class: ["book-toc-row"], children: kids }
//
// Three element constructors rather than one, because a WCL parameter
// list is fixed at declaration — the language has neither default nor
// named arguments, so every call fills every parameter and `id` /
// `attrs` each need a name of their own:
//
//   el(tag, cls, kids)          the common shape
//   ela(tag, cls, attrs, kids)  … with attributes
//   eli(tag, id, cls, kids)     … with an explicit HTML id
//
// plus four leaves — `raw` (verbatim HTML), `inl` (a prose run through
// the inline-pattern engine), `icon`, and `para`.
//
// `eli`, `inl`, `icon` and `para` currently have no caller in this
// stdlib: every block that used them — `callout`, then `chapter_header`
// / `code` / `footnotes` / `p` / `text` — now lowers to the semantic
// content IR instead of building HTML. What is left here is the
// template layer, which is HTML by definition. They are not dead:
// `Element`, `Inline`, `Icon` and `Paragraph` are live variants any
// user `lower` may return, most of the element vocabulary's
// construction sites are OUTSIDE this stdlib (the docs landing page,
// the scaffold templates, the wskill projections), and all four are
// covered by `tests/el.rs`.
//
// `ela` and `eli` take the SAME number of arguments, so arity — the one
// positional mistake WCL catches — does not separate them: calling one
// where you meant the other drops the id or the attrs silently. Pick by
// what you are passing, and reach for the long form when an element
// needs both.
//
// An empty `class` / `attrs` list emits no attribute at all (not
// `class=""`), so `el("li", [], kids)` renders `<li>` — passing `[]`
// costs nothing over omitting the field. An optional field goes
// straight in: `el("p", t.class, kids)` with `t.class` unset renders
// exactly as an omitted `class` does. The parameters below cannot SAY
// so — `?` in a parameter list is a parse error, so every wdoc `fn`
// annotates its optionals as though they were required and lets the
// `none` flow through (see `icon_lead` in `flowchart.wcl`).
//
// SCOPE: the HTML element vocabulary ONLY. `SvgFundamental` and the
// semantic content IR keep the named-field literal, because they are
// field-shaped — a positional constructor over them is the same record
// with its field names deleted, and WCL checks argument ARITY but never
// argument TYPES. Transposing two of an SVG `Label`'s four
// interchangeable `f64`s would render silently wrong where the long
// form raises a shape mismatch, and the saving there is an order of
// magnitude smaller than it is here. `callout.wcl` is the worked
// example on the IR side: it names its fields.
//
// The long form stays legal everywhere, and is the escape hatch for a
// combination this family doesn't name — an element carrying both an id
// and attrs, a `Paragraph` with an id, a `Head`, a `Table`. These are
// plain `let` bindings, so they resolve by BARE NAME once a document
// does `import <wdoc.wcl>`; don't shadow one with a `let` of your own.

namespace wdoc

// `<tag class="…">children</tag>`.
let el = fn(tag: utf8, cls: list<utf8>, kids: list<HtmlFundamental>) -> HtmlFundamental
  HtmlFundamental::Element { tag: tag, class: cls, children: kids }

// … with attributes — `attrs` is a list of `[name, value]` pairs, e.g.
// `[["href", p.href]]` (values escaped).
let ela = fn(tag: utf8, cls: list<utf8>, attrs: list<list<utf8>>, kids: list<HtmlFundamental>) -> HtmlFundamental
  HtmlFundamental::Element { tag: tag, class: cls, attrs: attrs, children: kids }

// … with an explicit HTML id (the anchor target).
let eli = fn(tag: utf8, id: identifier, cls: list<utf8>, kids: list<HtmlFundamental>) -> HtmlFundamental
  HtmlFundamental::Element { tag: tag, id: id, class: cls, children: kids }

// Verbatim, pre-rendered HTML — NOT escaped. Embeds already-rendered
// content (a page's `content`, an icon's SVG markup) into a template.
let raw = fn(html: utf8) -> HtmlFundamental
  HtmlFundamental::Raw { html: html }

// An inline prose run: fed through the inline-pattern engine, so
// `**bold**` / `` `code` `` / `[links](url)` / `:icons:` / `$math$` all
// apply. Wrap it yourself (`el("p", [], [inl(txt)])`).
let inl = fn(text: utf8) -> HtmlFundamental
  HtmlFundamental::Inline { text: text }

// An icon, resolved against the declared `iconset`s and the built-in
// packs (`pack.name`, e.g. `"lucide.info"`).
let icon = fn(name: utf8, cls: list<utf8>) -> HtmlFundamental
  HtmlFundamental::Icon { name: name, class: cls }

// A `<p>` of `spans`, each run through the inline-pattern engine. A
// paragraph that needs an id writes the long form.
let para = fn(cls: list<utf8>, spans: list<utf8>) -> HtmlFundamental
  HtmlFundamental::Paragraph { class: cls, spans: spans }
